				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607504850" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607335397 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Dec 2020 10:03</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4838596">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm going to be honest: this draft, in this state, is not a very interesting read. I would almost certainly downvote this if you posted it to the main site.</p>
<p>The core problem, for me, is that this is fundamentally a story of the conflict between D-32910 and Dr. Clyde, which is a fine idea, but it's barely explored. Like, the conflict fundamentally begins 2000 odd words in, with the extremely brief incident log; which is far too far in for the core plot to kick off. Well, there's technically a brief mention of it about 1.5k words in, at the end of the third sentence, but it's in an offhand comment Dr. Moore makes where we don't really have context for why he's mad at Clyde.</p>
<p>The conflict is then introduced, escalates and is resolved in ~1400 words and it's like&#8230;too shallow? Part of the problem is that we never really get a feel for what any of the characters are like as people, and so we don't really relate to them; and so their arguments aren't really that interesting. The rest of the problem is that the actual conflict is completely unrelatedable: we're given a few extremely brief snippets into it before the final confrontation and like,,,that confrontation is fine I guess but it doesn't feel climactic enough because the build up just isn't there.</p>
<p>It also feels really lacking in resolution: this feels like it should be a messy character drama with a more impactful ending than a quiet note saying 'Dr. Clyde was fired' followed by a weird note saying 'Dr. Moore was promoted'. In particular, the Moore promotion doesn't land cos he's almost completely a side character.</p>
<p>The complexity of the SCP feels&#8230;.awkward here; like&#8230;it kinda explains why there's so much testing but none of the testing seems to really go anywhere or produce any kind of results. Like, 90% of the experiments here are 'we gave them metal, they melted it, we nodded and said very good' and there's a few times they send drones in but it really just&#8230;shows that the termites behave like termites? So like the actual tests just aren't very interesting, and it wouldn't necessarily be an issue but it serves to highlight that the character interactions aren't interesting either.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sixteen SCP-XXXX-A instances (Designated as SCP-XXXX-A1 to A16) are kept at Site-XX high threat entomology wing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'Designated' shouldn't be capitalised here; give Site-XX a number and 'kept in the' would be neater phrasing.</p>
<p>You also never actually define what -A is; the implication from later is that they're individual colonies but?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Each SCP-XXXX-A instance is kept in a 15m X 15m X 15m tungsten lined chamber.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this is a really big room; does it need to be this size? does it need to be tungsten?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Heat shielded monitoring equipment <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">are</span><span style="color: green">is</span> set up to alert onsite personnel of temperature increase <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">of</span><span style="color: green">by</span> 50&#160;C or higher.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Generally for groups defined using a singular noun (e.g. 'equipment', you use 'is' instead of 'are'.</p>
<p>Also celsius uses degrees: &quot;50°C&quot; is the correct notation here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The following raw materials are to be provided monthly via an automated delivery system:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm&#8230;not really a fan of this list? There's one interesting item (scrap metal) but the rest feels like very generic stuff you'd give insects to keep 'em alive</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following incident.XXXX.15, no form of radioactive material is permitted for testing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Generally if you're referring to a specific incident, you'd capitalise Incident</p>
<blockquote>
<p>New testing materials may be requisitioned upon approval <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">of</span><span style="color: green">by the</span> Site-XX director.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Since you haven't established any standard testing material, this feels&#8230;vague. Like in universe, a reader doesn't know if a material is a new testing material or not from this, and out-of-universe, it doesn't hint at anything beyond 'there is testing occurring' which we've already seen from the list and the previous sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Only <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">the</span> approved personnel are to given access to internal containment chambers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What are the 'internal containment chambers'? Are you trying to imply 'access to the interior of the containment chambers' actually, instead of 'there are outer and inner containment chambers'?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the event of a containment breach the containment cell is to be flooded with a respiratory insecticide.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The paragraph this is attached to is largely about personnel: I'd probably move this somewhere else, perhaps to the end of the first paragraph which deals with the physical containment.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>embedded media personnel</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'personnel embedded in media organisations' would be a better way to phrase this to not imply 'personnel who are embedded media'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Neutralization is authorized should this prove impractical.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this feels vague about why movement would be impractical; I'm also questioning whether this much detail about moving stuff is necessary for the reader</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX differs in their large size and distinct coloration.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>differs from what? each other? general Isoptera?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Adult workers average 40cm to 65cm. Soldiers range from 80cm to 90cm without body modifications. Their base color is a dark brown with red highlights.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is excessively note-y imo; I'd probably try and mention the colour shortly after the colouration mention, and then talk about the adult workers and soldiers.</p>
<p>Also, average 40cm to 65cm in what? length? height? width?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1 takes the form of a viscus fluid at room temperature.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I believe you mean 'viscous' (as in thick) here</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX can heat and maintain its internal temperature at around 2000c. How SCP-XXXX generates or maintains this temperature is unknown.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is weirdly shoved into a paragraph that opens on the corrosive chemical produced by the SCP. I'd keep like ideas together.</p>
<p>Also see earlier comment about celsius. I'd probably not mention it being unknown and just leave it assumed.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If threatened, SCP-XXXX can expel SCP-XXXX-1 from its mouth or anus at high speed and distance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What is 'high speed and distance' here? A meter? 300 meters? A light year?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Causing severe burns and impact trauma.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sentence fragment: I'd probably leave this implied by it being corrosive and at high speed but *shrugs* your call; it at least needs to be e.g. &quot;This can cause severe burns&#8230;&quot; or tied into the previous sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX consumes a large verity of substance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I believe you mean 'variety' here? Also 'substances'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX has shown <span style="color: green">a</span># preference to <span style="color: green">consume plant matter</span> <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Plant mater</span>, wood, animal dung, gasoline, crude oil, and [REDACTED].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a sucky redaction. There's not really anything hinting at what it is yet so it doesn't make us curious or think in a direct line of anything specific, and so it's just kinda there.</p>
<p>Also why would the in-universe Foundation redact this? Surely it'd be important for anyone cleared to read this file to know what kinds of things might cause the SCP to come charging at them to eat.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX extracts carbon from its food and injects it into metals it consumes. This process results in high quality steel <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">even</span> regardless of the metals initial quality.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This phrasing implies that if it was fed say, pure aluminium, it would inject carbon into it to create a high carbon steel. You likely want to replace 'metal' with something more specific like 'iron'; though this would be a very basic understanding of metallurgy: 'high quality' is a vague term and often steels are alloyed with e.g. manganese or chromium for specific properties (e.g. improved strength or corrosion resistance) depending on need; and so 'high quality' doesn't necessarily mean 'more carbon'. Indeed, there's a point where you add too much carbon to steel and it gets all shitty again.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It was observed that SCP-XXXX instances became less aggressive to repeat<span style="text-decoration: line-through;">ed</span> visitors.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a wild thing to open an addendum with. It feels like this needs some context as to why there are repeat visitors in the first place and how it was detected.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following this, a D-Class personal (D-32910) was handpicked for an long term experiment. D-32910 was assigned to a colony (SCP-XXXX-A7) for in depth testing of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These sentences convey almost exactly the same idea and really it should be trimmed to one sentence that conveys both things.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-32910 was chosen due to their educational background and compliant behavior. SCP-XXXX-A7 was the chosen for testing as it was the first &quot;Foundation bred&quot; colony. This was to reduce possible cross contamination from previous testing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not really sure either of these things are necessary; particularly with the D-32910 dossier immediately after this. Though, I guess the conterpoint is cut the dossier instead because it doesn't really convey anything new beyond this one sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dossier: D-32910</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the personal information of the D-Class is important enough to be attached to the file, it is too important to redact <em>any</em> of it. My inclination though is to cut this completely.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Experiment.XXXX.A7.1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not really a fan of the tables here. They're very crushed: both between the tables since there's no empty lines and inside the tables since there's big blocks of text with no line breaks. A vertical quoteblock with the left column's headers would be more effective.</p>
<p>I'd also establish a standard test protocol before jumping into these so you don't have to cover the same 'd-class go in, puts test material down, observes for 30mins and leaves' info in all of these.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>10/16/20██</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why is the year redacted here? How does knowing if it took place in 2001 or 2009 cause a security breach?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-32910 will stay in the containment chamber for 30 minutes to make observations. If SCP-XXXX instances become aggressive</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the instances are aggressive, why is the D class being told to stay in the containment chamber at all? This feels like an unnecessary risk, particularly since they're trying to get the SCP to be more accustomed to the D-Class which they obviously can't do if they die.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-32910 is to document their notes in the provided terminal.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why don't they just record the test using cctv and then they don't need this to be done at all cos they can see what happened</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-32910 entered the containment chamber and approaches SCP-XXXX-A7.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We can assume that if the test protocol says this is to be the case, then they will do this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>At <span style="color: green">this</span> point members of SCP-XXXX-A7 notices D-32910 and take an aggressive stance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Aggressive stance should lead to D-32910 being signalled to stop; the information is backwards here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX instances melted and consumed it entirely.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this phrasing implies the SCP-XXXX instances melted, not that they melted the gold. Something like &quot;SCP-XXXX instances melted the gold before consuming it entirely.&quot; would be clearer.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-32910 remained in the containment chamber for the remainder of the time while keeping their distance as instructed. SCP-XXXX instances remained pacified for the remainder of the test.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd try to restructure this to avoid the repetition of 'remained'/'remainder'</p>
<p>To be honest, I would cut this bit; it can be assumed from the Protocol and it means you can focus on the interesting bits as opposed to 'the test went according to protocol'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>more like wasps or hornets than termites. Also, can I get some cooling in that suit?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>no suit was mentioned. Missing capital at the start of the first sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The instance seen above has been given the tentative title of &quot;Director&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why? There was no indication that that would fit their role.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Clyde connected to the device remotely <span style="color: green">and</span> performed standard diagnostics.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels like it gets into unnecessary levels of detail to convey 'we setup a probe'. I'm also unclear on why they didn't pilot the probe into the nest: it feels like having the D-Class set it up in there is unnecessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Slight static is heard from the probes microphones.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay? I don't know why this is mentioned.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After 5 minutes the gold has been formed into <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">a near</span> a cube roughly 7mm in size.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is&#8230;tiny? Like these things are 40cm long so it feels&#8230;odd for them to make the cube this small?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A Director instance similar to the one seen in Experiment.XXXX.A7.2. Approaches</p>
</blockquote>
<p>unnecessary capitalisation of 'approaches'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>to trail the instance; However further progress</p>
</blockquote>
<p>unnecessary capitalisation of 'however'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The probe packed by D-32910. D-32910 exits the containment chamber with the RLSOP.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd cut this, we can assume the probe is removed successfully.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Director instances seem disregard this and take materials by force.” -Dr. Clyde</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Every other time, you bolded the name of the people here. I would probably leave them unbolded tbh.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While much older workers perform more complex tasks.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sentence fragment that should be part of the previous sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Workers will provide food and gifts to older members in exchange for goods or services.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We don't need a list of examples here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You have 0 unread emails</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd put the email into a quote block to clearly segment it as a thing. The <a href="/how-to-write-an-scp">How to Write an SCP</a> guide shows how to do it using divs in the 'Interview Template' in the 'Templates' tab; which is far easier than using &gt;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon approval from the site director, we have been given approval for D-32910 to assist in lab testing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Repetition of them getting approval is unnecessary</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In addition D-32910 has been approved for an additional 2 hours a day <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">for</span><span style="color: green">of</span> personal testing on individual SCP-XXXX instances if desired, given that the following requirements are met:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'Additional' implies the D-class was getting personal testing time already; the D-Class should not under any circumstances be getting personal testing time.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Any Insubordination, aggression, misbehavior</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Insubordination should not be capitalised here</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Site-19 Site-XX high threat entomology wing</p>
</blockquote>
<p>repeated site name</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Over a four-month period D-32910, performed over ███ personal tests.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>comma should be after period, not where it is. This is a wack redaction too.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Incident.XXXX.27 VIDEO LOG</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This incident conveys so little to the reader that it's not worth keeping it in this state. It really needs to highlight why the altercation happened so that the Post Incident interview following it can build off that rather than just 'there is some kind of incident' followed by 'please exposit everything that happened'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Post incident interview - D-32910</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd add an empty line between each line of dialogue. Using &quot;&gt; &quot; (note the space after the &gt;) on a line will allow an empty line to be added.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&lt;Begin Log, 3/5/20██ 13:54&gt;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>yet another bad redaction.</p>
<p>question, should end in a question mark</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The door is heard opening and closing as Dr. Webers assistant enters the room and places a bottle of water on the table, followed by the door opening and closing once more as they leave.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd put actions like this in italics to distinguish them more from text.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I told him he was being a [expletive removed].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>you are allowed to swear in a scp article.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He began shouting at me, and then he brought up ████.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Since this is redacted, a reader's immediate reaction is 'wait what'. If this person had been given a name, we would be a lot more likely to realise what you mean here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Report.XXXX.21</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The use of styling here is actively distracting considering there's not really any reason for it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I will not and cannot allow personal altercations to impact the work done within Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The use of 'I will' makes this feel a lot less weighty in the judgement than a more passive construction like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Personal alteractions cannot be allowed to impact the work done within the Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>would convey.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Clyde has been reprimanded for unprofessional behavior and unauthorized disclosure of classified information.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure what 'unauthorized disclosure of classified information' is referring to like, the D-Class almost certainly already knows about them killing redacted so??</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Moore was absent due to being bitten by an SCP-████ instance thus requiring medical leave.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unnecessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>chamber of the nest. With the object.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The end sentence should be part of the previous sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Today is Dr. Moores last day on the SCP-XXXX project</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels like a weird place for the ending to go.</p>
</div>
